text:
Wow, I must say, Oppenheimer truly blew my mind! I know it's been said countless times by now, and I'm beating an old horse. In fact, some of you may even suspect that I'm doing this for upvotes. Still I can't hold my excitement about this film any longer. The initial hour took its time to set the stage, and the testimony setup between Oppenheimer and Strauss at different time periods had a distinct The Prestige vibe, which I found intriguing. Despite the deliberate pacing, the tension kept me on edge, eagerly awaiting the Trinity test. The actors delivered commendable performances, as expected.

&amp;#x200B;

The countdown to the Trinity explosion, after nearly 90 minutes, was an intense experience. The absence of sound during the explosion followed by the sudden, earth-shattering BOOM was worth every penny of my ticket.

&amp;#x200B;

Now, let's talk about Nolan and his characters. I'm a fan, but sometimes his characters feel kinda flat, except for Joker in The Dark Knight and, quite arguably, Leonard in Memento. However, Oppenheimer defies this notion. Nolan proves his ability to craft multidimensional characters with Oppenheimer's complex persona. The real weight of the film lies in the aftermath of the Trinity test and the consequences it brought, exposing Oppenheimer's fears and guilt, which resonates deeply with the audience. The harrowing gymnasium scene, with its blinding light and haunting imagery, like the glimpse of faces melting, walking through the ashes, hearing the wallowing of the lost families, just before he's pulled back into reality by a cheering crowd, will stay with me for a long time.

&amp;#x200B;

Robert Downey Jr.'s portrayal of Admiral Strauss was a pleasant surprise. He successfully sheds his Tony Stark persona to portray a vindictive and manipulative character. The revelation of Strauss's involvement in Oppenheimer's hearing and his attempt to discredit him adds another layer to the narrative, transforming the film into more than just a Manhattan Project origin story. Oppenheimer emerges as a multifaceted individual with ambitions and controversial beliefs, yet unwavering loyalty to his country.

&amp;#x200B;

Then it turns into a courtroom drama and man, I was on the edge, waiting to see how Oppenheimer would come out of this mess and get his security clearance back. Emily Blunt's defence at the hearing was epic, showing she's no damsel in distress. Alas, Teller's and Grover's testimonies turned the tables, and Oppenheimer's clearance got revoked, leading to the revocation of his security clearance and silencing his role as a nuclear weapons expert.

&amp;#x200B;

But wait, it's not over yet. Strauss faces his own hearing, and I'm thinking he's got this in the bag, but oh boy, was I wrong! Rami Malek nails it as David Hall, exposing Strauss for the snake he is. I never thought I'd cheer against RDJ, but there I was, grinning when he lost his shot at Secretary.

&amp;#x200B;

The movie concludes with a poignant discussion between Oppenheimer and Einstein, hinted at from the beginning. Einstein's wisdom regarding Oppenheimer's life after his fall from grace strikes a bittersweet chord, leaving a lasting impression. The final moments of the film, with Oppenheimer's contemplative expression as he envisions a world advancing in nuclear weapons, provide a powerful and chilling conclusion.

&amp;#x200B;

Okay, I know this is getting long, but let's talk about some other stuff. The sound design was top-notch, not Hans Zimmer level, but still sublime. I'm glad they didn't go all out like Tenet; they kept it restrained and it worked. That drumbeat during Oppenheimer's hearing, overpowering everything else, was a perfect reflection of his troubled mind. And the Trinity scene, no sound at first, then the explosion hit you like a freight train. Brilliant!

&amp;#x200B;

Finally, the dialogue. Nolan's films sometimes suffer from expository, dry dialogue, but not this time. Oppenheimer's chats with Einstein, Emily Blunt's defence, and her toughening up of Oppenheimer – all had some fantastic subtexts. Nolan's best dialogue yet!

